# Agency OS — environment template.
# Copy to .env at the REPO ROOT and fill in. This file is committed: never put a
# real value in it. Variable names and grouping mirror PLAN.md §8 exactly.
#
# SETUP.md is the procedure that fills this file in, in the order that each value
# unblocks the next, with a way to check each one. This file says what a variable
# is; SETUP.md says where to get it and how to know it is right.
#
# One .env at the root serves both apps:
#   - apps/worker loads it via `tsx --env-file-if-exists=../../.env`
#   - apps/web loads it in next.config.ts (Next.js only reads apps/web/.env itself)
#   - the operator scripts load it via `tsx --env-file-if-exists=.env`
# In production there is no file: Vercel and Railway supply these as platform
# variables, and a platform variable always wins over the file.
#
# Comments live on their own line, never after a value — a trailing comment ends
# up inside the value in some .env parsers.
#
# Step 6 (Today page, approve, Gmail send) added no variable to this file. It is
# recorded here because the absence is a decision and not an oversight: the send
# path uses GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID, GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET, GMAIL_REFRESH_TOKEN and the
# three compliance-footer values, all of which were already listed below, and it
# is not permitted to invent a sender address — users.messages.send fills From
# from the authenticated Gmail account, so there is no such variable to set.
#
# Steps 7 and 9 added none either. Step 7 (notifications) uses the VAPID pair
# already listed, and takes its VAPID `subject` from ALLOWED_EMAIL rather than
# adding a fourth name for the same address. Step 9 (edit-as-training-data) reads
# only from the database.
#
# Step 8 (the inbox agent) added two, both under Gmail below:
# GMAIL_WEBHOOK_AUDIENCE and GMAIL_PUBSUB_SERVICE_ACCOUNT. They are a PLAN §8
# amendment — the plan listed GMAIL_WEBHOOK_SECRET and stopped there, and a shared
# secret in a URL is not enough on its own. It appears in the Pub/Sub subscription
# config, in any proxy log that records query strings, and in shell history; it
# proves the caller once knew a secret. The OIDC token proves the caller is that
# Google service account, right now, signed by Google, and expiring. Both are
# checked, in that order, because the secret costs one hash and no I/O.

# --- Supabase ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Project URL from Supabase → Settings → API. Web + worker. Step 1.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
# Public anon key, safe in the browser, RLS applies. Web. Step 1.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# Worker and operator scripts only — bypasses RLS, never expose to the browser. Steps 1, 4, 5.
#
# NEVER SET THIS ON THE VERCEL PROJECT. It belongs to the Railway worker and to a
# laptop running the operator scripts, and nowhere else. It bypasses row-level
# security completely: anything holding it can read and write every contact, every
# draft and every setting regardless of who is signed in. The web app is built to
# work without it — apps/web/lib/supabase/server.ts uses the anon key under RLS on
# purpose, and next.config.ts's WEB_ENV_ALLOWLIST deliberately does not carry this
# name — so setting it on Vercel would add no capability the app uses and would put
# the one key that defeats every access rule into a process that renders HTML to a
# browser.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- Auth -------------------------------------------------------------------

# The single email allowed to sign in via magic link; checked in Next.js middleware. Step 2.
ALLOWED_EMAIL=

# --- Anthropic --------------------------------------------------------------

# Anthropic API key used by packages/core/src/llm.ts. Step 4.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# --- Gmail ------------------------------------------------------------------

# OAuth client ID for the Gmail API (Google Cloud project). Step 6.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# OAuth client secret paired with GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID. Step 6.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Long-lived refresh token for the owner's Gmail account; re-auth = replace this. Step 6.
GMAIL_REFRESH_TOKEN=
# Pub/Sub topic for Gmail users.watch push notifications. Step 8.
#
# The FULL RESOURCE NAME, not the topic's short name:
#   projects/<project-id>/topics/<topic-name>
# users.watch rejects a bare name. And the topic needs one IAM binding that has
# nothing to do with this file and is the most likely first-run failure of the
# whole system: roles/pubsub.publisher for gmail-api-push@system.gserviceaccount.com,
# which is Gmail itself. Without it users.watch answers 403 — and a 403 from Gmail
# reads like a scope problem, which is what it will look like you have.
GMAIL_PUBSUB_TOPIC=
# Shared secret authenticating the Pub/Sub → worker webhook. Step 8.
#
# Any high-entropy string; it is compared in constant time against the `token`
# query parameter on the webhook URL. `openssl rand -hex 32` is fine. It has to
# be the same value here and in the subscription's push endpoint, which is the
# only place it appears in a URL.
GMAIL_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# The `aud` claim on the OIDC token Pub/Sub sends. Step 8.
# Without checking it, a Google-signed token minted for any other service verifies
# here perfectly.
#
# EXACTLY the audience string configured on the push subscription — the route
# compares it verbatim and derives nothing. The webhook URL is the conventional
# choice, but "conventional" is not "computed": whatever you typed into the
# subscription is what goes here, character for character.
GMAIL_WEBHOOK_AUDIENCE=
# The `email` claim: the service account Pub/Sub signs deliveries as. Step 8.
# Without it, any holder of a Google account with the right audience passes.
GMAIL_PUBSUB_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=
#
# Both are checked in apps/worker/src/routes/gmail-webhook.ts. If either is unset
# the route refuses every delivery with 503 rather than running unauthenticated:
# an open webhook that creates contacts and spends Anthropic money is not a thing
# that ships on by default. Local work uses GET /jobs/inbox/run instead, which is
# loopback-only and drives the identical code path with no webhook at all.
#
# One value change with no variable to represent it, and it will 403 everything:
# GMAIL_REFRESH_TOKEN above was minted for gmail.send. Step 8 also calls
# history.list, messages.get, getProfile and users.watch, which need
# https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.readonly. The token must be reissued with
# that scope added. Nothing in the code can catch this — it is a value change, not
# a name change — so it surfaces as a 403 on the first read.

# --- Web Push ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Generate the pair with the dependency the worker already carries:
#   pnpm --filter @agency/worker exec web-push generate-vapid-keys
# One pair, generated once. Regenerating it invalidates every row in
# push_subscriptions, because a subscription is bound to the public key it was
# created with — every browser has to press "Enable notifications" again.

# VAPID public key for the PWA push subscription. Step 7.
#
# The ONE variable deliberately set on BOTH deploys: Railway needs it to sign, and
# Vercel needs it because apps/web reads it server-side in /brain and hands it to
# the subscribe button as a prop. It is safe on both because it is a public key —
# that is what "application server key" means; the browser receives it in the
# clear on every subscribe call. It is also in next.config.ts's WEB_ENV_ALLOWLIST,
# which is the only reason the web app can see it at all.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID private key used by the worker to sign push messages. Step 7.
#
# NEVER SET THIS ON THE VERCEL PROJECT. Railway only. Anything holding it can push
# a notification to the operator's lock screen as this application, and the web app
# has no use for it whatsoever — it signs nothing.
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=

# --- Compliance footer (GDPR — all four elements required) ------------------
#
# Worker only, and all three are asserted before a message is built — including
# under DRY_RUN, because a dry run whose only difference from production is that
# it would have failed is not a dry run. An unset value here refuses the send
# rather than sending a footer with a hole in it: the address is a legal
# requirement on every outbound message in the EU, and a placeholder would be a
# false statement to a customer.

# Sender identity shown in the footer of every outbound email. Step 6.
BUSINESS_NAME=
# Physical address, required on every outbound email. Step 6.
BUSINESS_ADDRESS=
# Public URL of the privacy policy, linked in the footer — must resolve to a real page. Step 6.
PRIVACY_POLICY_URL=

# --- Behaviour --------------------------------------------------------------

# Default true in development — agents run, tasks are created, Gmail send is stubbed. Steps 5, 6.
#
# ONE FLAG, THREE STUBS, and they interact. It gates the Gmail send
# (packages/core/src/gmail.ts), the users.watch registration
# (packages/core/src/gmail-read.ts) and Web Push (apps/worker/src/jobs/push.ts).
# So while it is true no watch is ever registered, which means Pub/Sub never
# delivers, which means the webhook path — the OIDC check, the shared secret, the
# immediate reply — is never exercised at all. There is no DRY_RUN_SEND /
# DRY_RUN_WATCH split: the watch stub exists because a developer's machine
# registering a watch would divert the production mailbox's notifications to
# whatever topic that developer had set, and a second flag is more configuration
# for a problem that already has an answer.
#
# The answer is settings.daily_send_cap = 0, which is a documented value meaning
# "sending is paused" — every send is refused with a sentence saying so while the
# inbox agent still reads, classifies and drafts. Set the cap to 0 FIRST, then
# DRY_RUN=false. SETUP.md Phase 9 is that order written out with its checks.
#
# What is NOT stubbed under dry run: drafting. followup.ts never consults this
# flag, so the morning run costs real Anthropic money in every mode. And a
# dry-run send still writes the interactions row, still marks the task `sent`,
# still consumes the daily cap and still trips the 48-hour per-contact cooldown —
# a draft approved in dry run can never be sent for real.
DRY_RUN=true
# Process timezone; all timestamps stored as UTC, local logic uses Europe/Athens. Step 1.
TZ=Europe/Athens
